module.exports = {
  title: 'Reactivity Deep Dive',
  slug: 'reactivity-deep-dive',
  order: 3,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'How Reactivity Works: Proxy, Effects, Track and Trigger',
      slug: 'reactivity-internals',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>This is the question that separates mid-level from senior in a Vue interview. Anyone can say "Vue 3 uses Proxy." Being able to describe the <em>dependency graph</em> — what gets recorded, where it is stored, what happens on a write, how the re-render is scheduled — demonstrates that you understand the framework rather than merely operate it.</p>`,
        `<p>It is also the most practically useful theory in Vue. Every reactivity bug you will ever debug — destructuring, replacing an object, a watcher that never fires, a computed that never updates — is explained by the same mechanism. Learn it once and those bugs stop being mysterious.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue's reactivity is a <strong>dependency-tracking system</strong> built from three pieces:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Reactive objects</strong> — Proxies (or refs) that intercept reads and writes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Effects</strong> — functions Vue runs and re-runs: a component's render function, a computed's getter, a watcher's callback.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>The dependency map</strong> — a record of which effects read which properties.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p>The loop is two operations with names taken straight from the source code:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>track()</code></strong> — on read: "the effect currently running depends on this property."</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>trigger()</code></strong> — on write: "re-run every effect that depends on this property."</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch4-track-trigger.svg|Diagram of the reactivity loop: an effect runs and reads a proxied property, the get trap calls track to record the dependency in a targetMap, a later write hits the set trap and calls trigger which queues the effect to re-run|The whole system: reads record dependencies, writes look them up and re-run the effects that care.}}`,
        `<p>Everything else in Vue's reactivity is a variation on this. A computed is an effect that caches its result. A watcher is an effect that calls your callback instead of rendering. A component is an effect that produces a virtual DOM tree.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>The Proxy.</strong> <code>reactive(obj)</code> returns <code>new Proxy(obj, handlers)</code> whose <code>get</code> handler calls <code>track()</code> before returning the value, and whose <code>set</code> handler calls <code>trigger()</code> after writing it. The handlers cover more than <code>get</code>/<code>set</code>: <code>has</code> (for <code>in</code>), <code>deleteProperty</code> and <code>ownKeys</code> (for <code>Object.keys</code> and <code>for…in</code>) are all trapped, which is why Vue 3 detects property addition and deletion and Vue 2 did not.</p>`,
        `<p>A <code>ref</code> is simpler: a class instance with a <code>value</code> accessor whose getter tracks and whose setter triggers. Objects assigned to a ref are passed through <code>reactive()</code>, which is why <code>user.value.name = 'x'</code> is tracked.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The active effect.</strong> Vue keeps a module-level variable, <code>activeEffect</code>. Before running an effect it sets that variable; after, it restores the previous one (a stack, so nested effects work). <code>track()</code> simply reads <code>activeEffect</code>. This is why a property read <em>outside</em> any effect records nothing — there is nobody to record it for — and it is the reason "reading state in an event handler doesn't create a dependency."</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The dependency map</strong> is a three-level structure:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-text">targetMap: WeakMap&lt;target, Map&lt;key, Set&lt;effect&gt;&gt;&gt;\n\n  { user: … }  →  'name'  →  { renderEffect, computedEffect }\n                  'email' →  { renderEffect }\n  { cart: … }  →  'items' →  { totalComputed }</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A <code>WeakMap</code> at the top means a reactive object that becomes garbage collectable takes its dependency entries with it — no leak. The per-key <code>Set</code> is what makes tracking <em>fine-grained</em>: writing <code>user.email</code> re-runs only the effects that read <code>email</code>, not everything touching <code>user</code>. That granularity is the core difference from React, which re-renders a component whenever its state changes regardless of which field you read.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Scheduling.</strong> <code>trigger()</code> does not run effects immediately — it pushes them onto a queue. The queue de-duplicates (an effect queued twice runs once) and flushes on the next microtask. So this runs one render, not three:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">state.a = 1\nstate.b = 2\nstate.c = 3     // ← one re-render, on the next microtask</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Within the flush, jobs are sorted by component id so parents update before children, which avoids a child rendering twice when a parent's change would have removed it anyway. <code>nextTick()</code> returns a promise that resolves after this flush — that is its entire definition.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Cleanup between runs.</strong> Before an effect re-runs, Vue removes it from the dependency sets it recorded last time, then re-tracks. That is what makes <em>conditional</em> dependencies correct: an effect reading <code>show.value ? a.value : b.value</code> depends on <code>a</code> or <code>b</code>, never both stale.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Vue 2 vs Vue 3.</strong> Vue 2 used <code>Object.defineProperty</code> to convert every property to a getter/setter at creation time. Consequences: it had to walk the whole object up front (slow for large objects); it could not see properties added later (<code>Vue.set</code>) or deleted (<code>Vue.delete</code>); it could not intercept <code>arr[0] = x</code> or <code>arr.length = 0</code>, so array methods were patched by hand; and Map/Set were invisible. <code>Proxy</code> intercepts operations rather than properties, so all of that works, and conversion is lazy — a nested object becomes reactive only when first read. The cost is that <code>Proxy</code> cannot be polyfilled, which is why Vue 3 dropped IE11.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>A minimal reactivity system — roughly a hundred lines less than Vue's, but the same shape. Being able to sketch this is a strong senior signal:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">let activeEffect = null\nconst targetMap = new WeakMap()\n\nfunction track(target, key) {\n  if (!activeEffect) return                     // nothing is watching\n  let depsMap = targetMap.get(target)\n  if (!depsMap) targetMap.set(target, (depsMap = new Map()))\n  let dep = depsMap.get(key)\n  if (!dep) depsMap.set(key, (dep = new Set()))\n  dep.add(activeEffect)\n}\n\nfunction trigger(target, key) {\n  const dep = targetMap.get(target)?.get(key)\n  if (!dep) return\n  // copy first: an effect may re-track and mutate the set while we iterate\n  ;[...dep].forEach(effect =&gt; effect())\n}\n\nfunction reactive(target) {\n  return new Proxy(target, {\n    get(obj, key, receiver) {\n      track(obj, key)\n      const result = Reflect.get(obj, key, receiver)\n      // deep, and LAZY — only converts what is actually read\n      return typeof result === 'object' &amp;&amp; result !== null ? reactive(result) : result\n    },\n    set(obj, key, value, receiver) {\n      const old = obj[key]\n      const result = Reflect.set(obj, key, value, receiver)\n      if (old !== value) trigger(obj, key)      // skip no-op writes\n      return result\n    },\n    deleteProperty(obj, key) {\n      const had = key in obj\n      const result = Reflect.deleteProperty(obj, key)\n      if (had) trigger(obj, key)                 // ← Vue 2 could not do this\n      return result\n    },\n  })\n}\n\nfunction effect(fn) {\n  const wrapped = () =&gt; {\n    activeEffect = wrapped\n    try { fn() } finally { activeEffect = null }\n  }\n  wrapped()                                      // run once to collect deps\n  return wrapped\n}\n\n// ── it works ──\nconst state = reactive({ count: 0, name: 'Ada' })\neffect(() =&gt; console.log('count is', state.count))  // logs 0\nstate.count = 5                                     // logs 5\nstate.name = 'Grace'                                // logs NOTHING — not a dependency</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A ref, for comparison — the same idea without a Proxy:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">function ref(initial) {\n  let value = typeof initial === 'object' &amp;&amp; initial !== null ? reactive(initial) : initial\n  const r = {\n    get value() { track(r, 'value'); return value },\n    set value(v) {\n      if (v === value) return\n      value = typeof v === 'object' &amp;&amp; v !== null ? reactive(v) : v\n      trigger(r, 'value')\n    },\n  }\n  return r\n}\n// This is why refs need .value — the property is the interception point.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Why the mechanism explains the classic bugs:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const state = reactive({ count: 0 })\n\n// ❌ Destructuring: the get trap fires ONCE, copying the number out.\n//    Later increments never touch the proxy, so trigger() never runs.\nlet { count } = state\ncount++\n\n// ❌ Mutating the raw target: no proxy involved, no set trap, no trigger.\nconst raw = { n: 1 }\nconst prox = reactive(raw)\nraw.n = 2\n\n// ❌ Reading outside an effect: activeEffect is null, so track() returns early.\nconsole.log(state.count)      // records nothing\n\n// ✅ Reading inside an effect: activeEffect is set, dependency recorded.\nwatchEffect(() =&gt; console.log(state.count))\n\n// ❌ Reassigning the variable: your binding points elsewhere; every effect\n//    is still subscribed to the ORIGINAL proxy.\nlet s = reactive({ n: 1 })\ns = reactive({ n: 2 })</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Believing tracking is automatic everywhere.</strong> It only happens while an effect is running. A read in an event handler or a plain function records nothing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting the raw object to be reactive.</strong> <code>reactive(raw)</code> returns a different object; writes to <code>raw</code> bypass every trap.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Thinking updates are synchronous.</strong> They are queued and flushed on the next microtask — hence <code>nextTick</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming reads of untouched properties cost tracking.</strong> Conversion is lazy and per-key; the map only grows for what is actually read.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Comparing proxies with <code>===</code> against raw objects.</strong> <code>reactive(obj) !== obj</code>. Use <code>toRaw()</code> if you need identity, and be careful with <code>Set</code>/<code>Map</code> membership checks.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reactivity through a getter you forgot to call.</strong> Passing <code>props.value</code> to a watcher passes a snapshot; passing <code>() =&gt; props.value</code> passes something re-readable inside the effect.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating a large object in a loop and expecting one trigger.</strong> You get one <em>render</em> thanks to the queue, but each write still runs the set trap; for hot loops, build a new object and assign once.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Think in terms of "which effect read which property" — it makes every reactivity bug diagnosable instead of magical.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep reactive state as small and flat as is practical; deep structures are supported but harder to reason about and to diff.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>shallowRef</code>/<code>shallowReactive</code> for large payloads that are replaced wholesale — deep conversion of a 10,000-row array is real work.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>ref</code> so that "does this survive destructuring?" never has to be asked.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>markRaw</code> for things that must never be proxied: class instances from third-party libraries, chart objects, map instances, component definitions.</li>`,
        `<li>Batch related mutations in one function — the scheduler already collapses them into a single render.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for the Vue DevTools timeline when a component re-renders more than expected; it shows which dependency triggered it.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Explain Vue 3's reactivity system."</strong><br>A: Three parts. Reactive objects are Proxies whose <code>get</code> trap calls <code>track()</code> and whose <code>set</code> trap calls <code>trigger()</code>. Effects are the functions Vue re-runs — render functions, computed getters, watcher callbacks — and while one runs, Vue keeps a reference to it in <code>activeEffect</code>. <code>track()</code> records "this effect depends on this key" into a <code>WeakMap&lt;target, Map&lt;key, Set&lt;effect&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>; <code>trigger()</code> looks up that set and queues those effects. The queue de-duplicates and flushes on the next microtask, so many writes produce one re-render.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why <code>Proxy</code> instead of <code>Object.defineProperty</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>defineProperty</code> converts individual properties, so Vue 2 had to walk the whole object up front, could not detect properties added or removed later — hence <code>Vue.set</code> and <code>Vue.delete</code> — could not intercept array index assignment or <code>length</code>, so array methods were monkey-patched, and could not track Map or Set at all. <code>Proxy</code> intercepts the <em>operations</em>, so all of that works naturally, and conversion is lazy so nested objects are only proxied when read. The trade-off is no polyfill, which is why Vue 3 dropped IE11.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is an effect?"</strong><br>A: A function Vue runs while tracking its reads, so it can re-run when those reads change. A component's render function is an effect; so is a computed getter and a watcher. The mechanism is uniform — the only difference is what the effect does when it re-runs: produce a vnode tree, recompute a cached value, or invoke your callback.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does a Set per key matter?"</strong><br>A: It makes tracking fine-grained. Because dependencies are recorded per property, writing <code>user.email</code> re-runs only effects that actually read <code>email</code> — a component that renders <code>user.name</code> is untouched. React re-renders the component on any state change and leaves it to you to prevent the waste with <code>memo</code> and friends. That difference is the substance behind "Vue's reactivity is fine-grained."</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Are DOM updates synchronous after a state change?" (trap)</strong><br>A: No. <code>trigger()</code> queues effects rather than running them; the queue de-duplicates and flushes on the next microtask, and jobs are ordered parent-before-child. So reading the DOM immediately after a state change gives you the previous frame. <code>await nextTick()</code> resolves after the flush, which is exactly when the DOM reflects the change.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Sketch a minimal reactivity system."</strong><br>A: A module-level <code>activeEffect</code>; a <code>WeakMap</code> from target to a <code>Map</code> from key to a <code>Set</code> of effects; <code>track(target, key)</code> that adds <code>activeEffect</code> to that set if one is running; <code>trigger(target, key)</code> that iterates a copy of the set and calls each effect; a <code>reactive()</code> returning a Proxy whose get calls track and whose set calls trigger; and an <code>effect(fn)</code> that sets <code>activeEffect</code>, runs <code>fn</code> once to collect dependencies, then restores it. Add lazy deep conversion in the get trap and you have the shape of the real thing.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Read → <code>track()</code> · Write → <code>trigger()</code>. That's the whole loop.</li>`,
        `<li><code>targetMap: WeakMap&lt;target, Map&lt;key, Set&lt;effect&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> — WeakMap so GC'd objects free their deps.</li>`,
        `<li><code>activeEffect</code> is set only while an effect runs ⇒ reads outside effects track nothing.</li>`,
        `<li>Effects = render function · computed getter · watcher callback. Same machinery.</li>`,
        `<li>Per-key <code>Set</code> ⇒ <strong>fine-grained</strong> updates (vs React's subtree re-render).</li>`,
        `<li>Triggers are <strong>queued</strong>, de-duplicated, flushed next microtask, parent before child. <code>nextTick()</code> awaits that flush.</li>`,
        `<li>Deps are cleared and re-collected each run ⇒ conditional dependencies stay correct.</li>`,
        `<li>Proxy traps: <code>get</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>has</code>, <code>deleteProperty</code>, <code>ownKeys</code> ⇒ add/delete/array/Map all work.</li>`,
        `<li>Vue 2's <code>defineProperty</code>: eager walk, no add/delete, patched array methods, no Map/Set.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Computed Properties',
      slug: 'computed',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>"Computed or method?" is asked in every Vue interview at every level, and the answer — caching — is only half of it. The interesting follow-ups are about <em>when</em> the cache invalidates, why a computed with a side effect is a bug, and what a writable computed is for. Those separate people who know the rule from people who know the reason.</p>`,
        `<p>In practice, computed properties are the main tool for keeping templates readable and renders cheap. A template littered with filtering and formatting logic re-does that work on every unrelated re-render; the same logic in a computed runs only when its inputs change.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A <strong>computed property</strong> is a value <em>derived</em> from other reactive state, and <strong>cached</strong> until that state changes.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const first = ref('Ada')\nconst last = ref('Lovelace')\nconst full = computed(() =&gt; \`\${first.value} \${last.value}\`)\n\nfull.value   // 'Ada Lovelace' — computed now\nfull.value   // 'Ada Lovelace' — from cache, getter NOT re-run\nfirst.value = 'Grace'\nfull.value   // 'Grace Lovelace' — recomputed on this access</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The contrast with a method is the whole point:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th></th><th>computed</th><th>method</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Cached?</td><td>Yes — until a dependency changes</td><td>No — runs on every access</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Re-runs on unrelated re-render?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Takes arguments?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Used as</td><td>A value: <code>{{ total }}</code></td><td>A call: <code>{{ total() }}</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Right for</td><td>Derived state</td><td>Actions, or derivation needing arguments</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The rule that follows: <strong>if it is a value derived from state, use a computed; if it is an action or needs arguments, use a method.</strong></p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>A computed is an effect with two extra pieces: a cached value and a <em>dirty</em> flag.</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li>On creation the getter does <strong>not</strong> run. Computeds are lazy — a computed nobody reads never executes.</li>`,
        `<li>On first <code>.value</code> access, the getter runs inside an effect, so every reactive property it reads is tracked. The result is cached and <em>dirty</em> is cleared.</li>`,
        `<li>Further reads return the cache with no work at all.</li>`,
        `<li>When a dependency is written, the computed is not recomputed — it is only marked <em>dirty</em>. The recomputation happens on the next read. This is why an expensive computed nobody is currently rendering costs nothing.</li>`,
        `<li>A computed is itself reactive: effects that read it are tracked against it, so a change propagates onward through chains of computeds.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `{{fig:ch4-computed-cache.svg|Diagram of a computed's lifecycle: lazy on creation, evaluated and cached on first read, marked dirty when a dependency changes, and recomputed only on the next access|A computed is lazy and cached: a dependency change only marks it dirty; the work happens on the next read.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Dependencies are collected dynamically</strong>, on every evaluation. A getter with a branch depends only on what it actually read this time:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const result = computed(() =&gt; (showDetails.value ? details.value : summary.value))\n// While showDetails is false, writing to details.value does NOT invalidate this.</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong>Writable computeds.</strong> Passing <code>{ get, set }</code> instead of a function gives a two-way derived value. The setter's job is to translate a write back onto the underlying sources. This is how you build a component-level <code>v-model</code> over a prop without mutating it, and how Vuex/Pinia-backed form fields are usually wired.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Purity is not a style preference.</strong> A getter must have no side effects — no API calls, no mutation of other state, no DOM writes. Two reasons: it may be called at unpredictable times (or not at all, since it is lazy), and mutating a dependency inside a getter creates a loop where computing the value invalidates the value. Side effects belong in <code>watch</code>/<code>watchEffect</code>; that is the division of labour between the two.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\n\nconst products = ref([])\nconst query = ref('')\nconst sortKey = ref('name')\nconst onlyInStock = ref(false)\n\n// Chained computeds: each step is cached independently, so changing sortKey\n// does NOT re-run the filtering.\nconst matching = computed(() =&gt;\n  products.value.filter(p =&gt; p.name.toLowerCase().includes(query.value.toLowerCase())),\n)\n\nconst available = computed(() =&gt;\n  onlyInStock.value ? matching.value.filter(p =&gt; p.stock &gt; 0) : matching.value,\n)\n\nconst sorted = computed(() =&gt;\n  // ⚠️ copy before sorting — sort() mutates, and mutating a dependency\n  //     inside a getter would re-trigger this computed forever.\n  [...available.value].sort((a, b) =&gt; String(a[sortKey.value]).localeCompare(String(b[sortKey.value]))),\n)\n\nconst total = computed(() =&gt; sorted.value.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p.price, 0))\nconst summary = computed(() =&gt; \`\${sorted.value.length} items · ₹\${total.value.toFixed(2)}\`)\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ summary }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;ProductRow v-for="p in sorted" :key="p.id" :product="p" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Writable computeds — the two patterns you will actually use:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { computed, ref } from 'vue'\n\n// ① Split/join a derived representation\nconst firstName = ref('Ada')\nconst lastName = ref('Lovelace')\n\nconst fullName = computed({\n  get: () =&gt; \`\${firstName.value} \${lastName.value}\`,\n  set: (value) =&gt; {\n    const [f, ...rest] = value.split(' ')\n    firstName.value = f\n    lastName.value = rest.join(' ')\n  },\n})\n\nfullName.value = 'Grace Hopper'   // writes through to both refs</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ② v-model over a prop, without mutating the prop --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { computed } from 'vue'\n\nconst props = defineProps({ modelValue: String })\nconst emit = defineEmits(['update:modelValue'])\n\nconst value = computed({\n  get: () =&gt; props.modelValue,\n  set: (v) =&gt; emit('update:modelValue', v),   // the parent still owns it\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;input v-model="value"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Caching, demonstrated — the reason the interview question exists:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst items = ref(bigArray)\nconst unrelated = ref(0)\n\nconst expensiveComputed = computed(() =&gt; {\n  console.log('computed ran')\n  return items.value.filter(heavyPredicate).length\n})\n\nfunction expensiveMethod() {\n  console.log('method ran')\n  return items.value.filter(heavyPredicate).length\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Rendered 3× each. Click the button (changing \`unrelated\`) and:\n       - \"computed ran\" logs 0 more times  (deps unchanged → cache serves all 3)\n       - \"method ran\"  logs 3 more times   (re-evaluated on every render)      --&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ expensiveComputed }} {{ expensiveComputed }} {{ expensiveComputed }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ expensiveMethod() }} {{ expensiveMethod() }} {{ expensiveMethod() }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;button @click="unrelated++"&gt;Re-render&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>What not to put in a getter:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Side effect — runs at unpredictable times, may never run, may run twice\nconst user = computed(() =&gt; {\n  fetch('/api/user')            // ← belongs in watch/watchEffect\n  analytics.track('viewed')     // ← same\n  return store.user\n})\n\n// ❌ Mutating a dependency inside the getter — self-invalidating loop\nconst sorted = computed(() =&gt; items.value.sort())      // sort() mutates in place\nconst sortedOk = computed(() =&gt; [...items.value].sort()) // ✅ copy first\n\n// ❌ Async — a computed must return a value, not a promise\nconst data = computed(async () =&gt; await fetch(url))     // .value is a Promise\n// ✅ Use watchEffect + a ref, or a data-fetching composable\n\n// ❌ Arguments — computeds take none\nconst byId = computed((id) =&gt; items.value.find(i =&gt; i.id === id))\n// ✅ Return a function (uncached, but correct) or use a method\nconst finder = computed(() =&gt; (id) =&gt; items.value.find(i =&gt; i.id === id))</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Side effects in the getter.</strong> The single most important rule. Getters may run at any time, or never, and mutating state inside one can loop.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating an array dependency.</strong> <code>.sort()</code>, <code>.reverse()</code> and <code>.splice()</code> mutate in place; copy with the spread operator (or <code>toSorted()</code>) first.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting a computed to accept arguments.</strong> It cannot. Return a function from it, or use a method — but note the returned function's results are not cached.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Async getters.</strong> <code>.value</code> will be a Promise. Use a watcher plus a ref, or a fetching composable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>.value</code> in script.</strong> A computed is a ref; in the template it auto-unwraps, in script it does not.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assigning to a read-only computed.</strong> Warns and does nothing; you need the <code>{ get, set }</code> form.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using a method for derived state.</strong> Works, but re-runs on every re-render including ones triggered by unrelated state.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming a computed re-runs the moment a dependency changes.</strong> It is marked dirty and recomputed on the next read — which is why a computed nobody renders costs nothing.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use computeds for anything derived and rendered; keep templates to a single reference.</li>`,
        `<li>Chain small computeds instead of writing one large one — each layer caches separately, so a change to sorting need not redo filtering.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep getters pure and cheap-ish; if a getter is genuinely expensive, that is fine, because caching is doing its job.</li>`,
        `<li>Name computeds as nouns describing the value (<code>visibleItems</code>, <code>orderTotal</code>), not as verbs.</li>`,
        `<li>Use writable computeds to wrap a prop or a store value for <code>v-model</code>, so you get two-way binding without mutating something you do not own.</li>`,
        `<li>Never mutate inside a getter — copy first when sorting or reversing.</li>`,
        `<li>Put side effects in <code>watch</code>/<code>watchEffect</code>; that separation is the whole design.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for <code>computed</code> in composables too — it is not a component-only tool.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "computed vs method?"</strong><br>A: A computed caches its result and only re-evaluates when one of its reactive dependencies changes; a method runs every time it is called, including on every re-render triggered by unrelated state. So referencing a computed three times in a template evaluates once, while calling a method three times evaluates three times. Computeds cannot take arguments and are used as values; methods can and are called. Rule: derived state → computed, actions or argument-dependent derivation → method.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does the caching actually work?"</strong><br>A: A computed is an effect with a cached value and a dirty flag. It is lazy — the getter does not run until something reads <code>.value</code>. On that first read it runs inside an effect so its reads are tracked, caches the result and clears dirty. Subsequent reads return the cache. When a dependency is written, the computed is only <em>marked</em> dirty; the recomputation happens on the next access. And because dependencies are re-collected on each run, a getter with a branch depends only on what it actually read.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why must a computed getter be side-effect free?"</strong><br>A: Because you do not control when it runs. It is lazy, so it may not run at all; it may run at an arbitrary later point when something reads it; and it can run again after any dependency change. A fetch or an analytics call in there fires unpredictably. Worse, mutating a dependency inside the getter invalidates the computed as it computes, which loops. Side effects belong in <code>watch</code>/<code>watchEffect</code> — that split is the point of having both.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's a writable computed for?"</strong><br>A: A two-way derived value. You pass <code>{ get, set }</code>, and the setter translates the write back onto the sources. Two common uses: splitting or joining a representation, like a <code>fullName</code> that writes back to <code>firstName</code> and <code>lastName</code>; and wrapping a prop for <code>v-model</code> — the getter returns <code>props.modelValue</code> and the setter emits <code>update:modelValue</code>, giving you two-way binding without ever mutating a prop.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Can a computed be async?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Not usefully. An async getter returns a Promise, so <code>.value</code> is a Promise, not the data. Computeds are for synchronous derivation. For async, use a watcher or <code>watchEffect</code> that writes into a <code>ref</code>, or a data-fetching composable that exposes <code>data</code>, <code>loading</code> and <code>error</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "I need a derived value that depends on an argument — <code>itemById(id)</code>."</strong><br>A: Computeds take no arguments, so either use a method, or return a function from a computed. The second keeps the reactive dependency on the source array but does not cache per-argument. If per-argument caching matters, compute a lookup Map once — <code>computed(() =&gt; new Map(items.value.map(i =&gt; [i.id, i])))</code> — and index into it, which is cached <em>and</em> O(1).</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>computed(getter)</code> → cached derived value; re-evaluates only when a dependency changes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Lazy</strong>: getter doesn't run until read. A dependency change marks <em>dirty</em>; work happens on the next access.</li>`,
        `<li>Method = re-runs every render. Computed = cached. Derived value → computed.</li>`,
        `<li>Dependencies re-collected each run ⇒ branches only depend on what they read.</li>`,
        `<li>Getter must be <strong>pure</strong> — no fetch, no mutation, no DOM. Side effects → <code>watch</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Copy before <code>.sort()</code>/<code>.reverse()</code>; mutating a dependency self-invalidates.</li>`,
        `<li>No arguments, no async. Return a function, or use a Map lookup.</li>`,
        `<li>Writable: <code>computed({ get, set })</code> — prop + emit for <code>v-model</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>It's a ref: <code>.value</code> in script, auto-unwrapped in template.</li>`,
        `<li>Chain small computeds — each layer caches independently.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Watchers: watch vs watchEffect',
      slug: 'watchers',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Watchers are how you run <em>side effects</em> in response to state changes — fetching when an id changes, persisting to storage, syncing a third-party widget. The interview question is almost always the same pair: "watch vs watchEffect?" and "when would you use a watcher instead of a computed?" Both have crisp answers, and both are commonly answered badly.</p>`,
        `<p>Watchers are also where subtle bugs live: watching an object without <code>deep</code> and seeing nothing fire, watching a getter without realising the old value is the same object, or racing responses when a fast second request resolves before a slow first. Knowing the failure modes is more valuable than knowing the API.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue gives you two watchers, and the difference is <strong>how dependencies are declared</strong>.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>watch(source, callback)</code></strong> — <em>explicit</em>. You state what to watch. The callback receives the new and old values, and does not run until the source changes (unless you pass <code>immediate: true</code>).</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">watch(userId, async (newId, oldId) =&gt; {\n  user.value = await fetchUser(newId)\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong><code>watchEffect(callback)</code></strong> — <em>implicit</em>. It runs the callback immediately, tracks whatever reactive values it reads, and re-runs when any of them change. No source list, no old value.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">watchEffect(async () =&gt; {\n  user.value = await fetchUser(userId.value)   // dependency inferred from the read\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th></th><th><code>watch</code></th><th><code>watchEffect</code></th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>Explicit source</td><td>Inferred from reads</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Runs immediately?</td><td>No (opt in with <code>immediate</code>)</td><td>Yes, always</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Old value?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Lazy?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Precise control; needing the previous value</td><td>Effects with several dependencies you'd rather not list</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>And the rule that governs both: <strong>computed for deriving a value, watcher for performing an action.</strong> If you find yourself writing a watcher whose only job is to assign to another ref, you wanted a computed.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Both are effects — the same machinery as Chapter 4.1. <code>watchEffect</code> tracks reads during its run, exactly like a render function. <code>watch</code> wraps the source in a getter, runs <em>that</em> to collect dependencies, and calls your callback only when the resulting value changes.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Valid sources for <code>watch</code></strong>, and the trap in the middle:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">watch(count, cb)                    // a ref\nwatch(() =&gt; props.userId, cb)       // ✅ a getter — the standard form for props\nwatch(props.userId, cb)             // ❌ passes a VALUE — never fires\nwatch([a, b], ([na, nb]) =&gt; {})     // an array of sources\nwatch(reactiveObj, cb)              // a reactive object — implicitly deep</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong>Deep watching.</strong> A watcher on a <code>ref</code> holding an object fires only when the ref is <em>reassigned</em>, not when a nested property changes — because the ref's value is still the same object reference. <code>{ deep: true }</code> makes Vue traverse the whole structure and track every nested property, which is correct but costs a full traversal on every check. Watching a specific path with a getter is usually cheaper and clearer. A <code>reactive</code> object passed directly is deep by default.</p>`,
        `<p>Deep watching has a second sharp edge: <strong>with <code>deep: true</code>, <code>newValue</code> and <code>oldValue</code> are the same object</strong>, because the mutation happened in place. If you need the previous state, clone it yourself.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Flush timing</strong> decides when the callback runs relative to the DOM update:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>flush: 'pre'</code> (default) — before the component re-renders. The DOM still shows the old state.</li>`,
        `<li><code>flush: 'post'</code> — after the DOM has been patched. Use this when the callback reads or measures the DOM. <code>watchPostEffect()</code> is the shorthand.</li>`,
        `<li><code>flush: 'sync'</code> — immediately and synchronously on every change, unbatched. Rarely correct; it defeats the update queue.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch4-watch-flush.svg|Timeline showing a state change followed by pre-flush watchers, then the component re-render and DOM patch, then post-flush watchers, illustrating which of them can read the updated DOM|Flush timing decides whether your callback sees the old DOM (pre, the default) or the new one (post).}}`,
        `<p><strong>Cleanup and race conditions.</strong> The callback receives an <code>onCleanup</code> function (or you can use <code>onWatcherCleanup()</code> in 3.5+) that runs before the next invocation and on stop. This is how you cancel a stale request — without it, a slow first response can land after a fast second one and overwrite fresh data with old. Async watchers without cancellation are one of the most common real bugs in Vue apps.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Stopping.</strong> A watcher created during <code>setup</code> is bound to the component and stops automatically on unmount. One created asynchronously — inside a <code>setTimeout</code>, after an <code>await</code>, or outside a component — is <em>not</em>, and must be stopped manually with the returned handle, or scoped with <code>effectScope()</code>.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, watch, watchEffect } from 'vue'\n\nconst props = defineProps({ userId: Number })\nconst user = ref(null)\nconst query = ref('')\nconst filters = ref({ role: 'all', active: true })\n\n// ① Watch a prop — ALWAYS via a getter\nwatch(\n  () =&gt; props.userId,\n  async (newId, oldId) =&gt; {\n    if (newId === oldId) return\n    user.value = await api.getUser(newId)\n  },\n  { immediate: true },        // also run once on setup\n)\n\n// ② Multiple sources — the callback gets arrays\nwatch([query, () =&gt; filters.value.role], ([q, role], [oldQ, oldRole]) =&gt; {\n  refetch(q, role)\n})\n\n// ③ Deep — needed for nested mutation of an object held in a ref\nwatch(filters, (v) =&gt; {\n  localStorage.setItem('filters', JSON.stringify(v))\n}, { deep: true })            // ⚠️ newValue === oldValue here\n\n// ④ Watch a specific path instead — cheaper and gives a real old value\nwatch(() =&gt; filters.value.role, (role, prevRole) =&gt; {\n  console.log(\`role: \${prevRole} → \${role}\`)\n})\n\n// ⑤ watchEffect — dependencies inferred, runs immediately\nwatchEffect(() =&gt; {\n  document.title = user.value ? \`\${user.value.name} · Profile\` : 'Loading…'\n})\n\n// ⑥ post-flush — the callback needs the UPDATED DOM\nwatch(user, () =&gt; {\n  listEl.value.scrollTop = 0\n}, { flush: 'post' })\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Cancelling stale async work — the pattern that prevents the classic race:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Broken: type \"ab\" quickly and the response for \"a\" may land last,\n//    overwriting the results for \"ab\".\nwatch(query, async (q) =&gt; {\n  results.value = await api.search(q)\n})\n\n// ✅ onCleanup runs before the next invocation — abort the previous request\nwatch(query, async (q, _prev, onCleanup) =&gt; {\n  const controller = new AbortController()\n  onCleanup(() =&gt; controller.abort())\n\n  try {\n    results.value = await api.search(q, { signal: controller.signal })\n  } catch (e) {\n    if (e.name !== 'AbortError') error.value = e\n  }\n})\n\n// ✅ watchEffect takes the same cleanup argument\nwatchEffect((onCleanup) =&gt; {\n  const id = setInterval(() =&gt; poll(userId.value), 1000)\n  onCleanup(() =&gt; clearInterval(id))\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The mistake that turns a watcher into a worse computed:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ A watcher whose only job is to assign a derived value.\n//    Extra state, extra re-render, and it can go stale.\nconst fullName = ref('')\nwatch([first, last], () =&gt; {\n  fullName.value = \`\${first.value} \${last.value}\`\n}, { immediate: true })\n\n// ✅ It's derived state — that's a computed.\nconst fullName = computed(() =&gt; \`\${first.value} \${last.value}\`)</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Stopping watchers, and scoping a group of them:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { watchEffect, effectScope } from 'vue'\n\n// Auto-stopped: created during setup, bound to the component\nwatchEffect(() =&gt; console.log(count.value))\n\n// Manual stop — e.g. a one-shot watcher\nconst stop = watch(status, (s) =&gt; {\n  if (s === 'ready') { init(); stop() }\n})\n\n// ⚠️ NOT auto-stopped — created after an await, outside the setup context\nsetTimeout(() =&gt; {\n  const stop2 = watchEffect(() =&gt; log(count.value))   // must stop this yourself\n}, 100)\n\n// A scope disposes every effect inside it at once\nconst scope = effectScope()\nscope.run(() =&gt; {\n  watch(a, cb1)\n  watchEffect(cb2)\n})\nscope.stop()   // stops both</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Passing a value instead of a getter.</strong> <code>watch(props.userId, cb)</code> watches a number and never fires. Use <code>() =&gt; props.userId</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting nested changes without <code>deep</code>.</strong> A ref holding an object fires only on reassignment.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming <code>oldValue</code> is useful with <code>deep: true</code>.</strong> It is the same object as <code>newValue</code>; clone if you need the previous state.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using a watcher to derive a value.</strong> That is a computed with extra state and an extra render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No cleanup on async watchers.</strong> Out-of-order responses overwrite fresh data. Abort in <code>onCleanup</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading the DOM in a default-flush watcher.</strong> The callback runs <em>before</em> the re-render, so you see the old DOM. Use <code>flush: 'post'</code> or <code>await nextTick()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Creating watchers asynchronously and never stopping them.</strong> They outlive the component. Keep the handle, or use <code>effectScope</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing a watched value inside its own watcher.</strong> Infinite loop unless you guard the condition.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for <code>watchEffect</code> when the trigger set matters.</strong> It re-runs on <em>any</em> read changing, including ones you added later without thinking.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Ask "am I deriving a value or performing an action?" first. Only actions need a watcher.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer <code>watch</code> with an explicit source when the trigger matters; it makes the dependency obvious to the next reader.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>watchEffect</code> for effects with several dependencies where listing them adds noise — updating a document title, syncing a canvas.</li>`,
        `<li>Always pass props as getters.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer watching a specific path over <code>deep: true</code>; you get cheaper checks and a genuine old value.</li>`,
        `<li>Always clean up async work with <code>onCleanup</code> and an <code>AbortController</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>flush: 'post'</code> whenever the callback touches the DOM.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep callbacks short — call a named function rather than inlining a block.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>effectScope()</code> in composables that create several effects outside a component's lifetime.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "watch vs watchEffect?"</strong><br>A: How dependencies are declared. <code>watch</code> takes an explicit source, is lazy, and gives you both new and old values — good when you care exactly what triggered it or need the previous value. <code>watchEffect</code> runs immediately, infers its dependencies from whatever it reads during that run, and has no old value — good for effects with several dependencies where listing them is noise. Both re-run on change and both support cleanup.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "computed or watcher?"</strong><br>A: Computed for deriving a value, watcher for performing an action. If you are producing a value from other state, that is a computed — cached, no extra state, no extra render. If you are reacting to a change by doing something with the outside world — fetching, persisting, logging, syncing a third-party widget — that is a watcher. A watcher whose only body is <code>x.value = f(y.value)</code> should have been a computed.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why doesn't my watcher fire when I change a nested property?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because the source is a ref holding an object, and the ref's value — the object reference — did not change; only a property inside it did. Add <code>{ deep: true }</code> to traverse and track nested properties, or better, watch the specific path with a getter, which is cheaper and gives a real old value. Note that with <code>deep: true</code>, <code>newValue</code> and <code>oldValue</code> are the same object because the mutation was in place.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you avoid race conditions in an async watcher?"</strong><br>A: Cancel the previous run. The callback receives an <code>onCleanup</code> function that fires before the next invocation and on stop; register an <code>AbortController.abort()</code> there and pass the signal to <code>fetch</code>. Without it, typing quickly in a search box can have the response for "a" arrive after the response for "ab" and overwrite it — the data is wrong and nothing errors.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is flush timing?"</strong><br>A: When the callback runs relative to the DOM update. Default <code>'pre'</code> runs before the component re-renders, so the DOM still shows the old state. <code>'post'</code> runs after the patch, which is what you need to measure or scroll an element. <code>'sync'</code> runs immediately on every change with no batching and is almost never right. <code>watchPostEffect</code> and <code>watchSyncEffect</code> are shorthands.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Do watchers need to be stopped?"</strong><br>A: Not if they are created synchronously during setup — Vue binds them to the component instance and stops them on unmount. But one created after an <code>await</code>, inside a callback, or outside a component entirely is not tracked, so it leaks. Keep the returned stop handle, or wrap the group in <code>effectScope()</code> and call <code>scope.stop()</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>watch(src, cb)</code> — explicit, lazy, gives <code>(new, old)</code>. <code>watchEffect(cb)</code> — implicit deps, runs immediately, no old value.</li>`,
        `<li>Sources: ref · getter · array · reactive object. <strong>Props always via getter.</strong></li>`,
        `<li><code>immediate: true</code> to run on setup; <code>once: true</code> (3.4+) for one-shot.</li>`,
        `<li><code>deep: true</code> for nested mutation — but <code>newValue === oldValue</code>. Prefer watching a path.</li>`,
        `<li>Flush: <code>pre</code> (default, old DOM) · <code>post</code> (new DOM) · <code>sync</code> (unbatched).</li>`,
        `<li><code>onCleanup</code> + <code>AbortController</code> ⇒ no stale-response races.</li>`,
        `<li>Created in setup → auto-stopped. Created async → stop it yourself or use <code>effectScope()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Deriving a value → computed. Doing something → watcher.</strong></li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Reactivity Utilities: toRef, toRaw, shallow, readonly, markRaw',
      slug: 'reactivity-utilities',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>These are the tools you reach for when the defaults are not what you want: when a reactive object must be destructured, when a huge array should not be deeply proxied, when a third-party class instance must be kept out of the reactivity system entirely, or when consumers of a store should be able to read but not write.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask about them because knowing <code>markRaw</code> exists implies you have hit the problem it solves — a chart library or a map instance behaving strangely because Vue proxied it. That is real experience, and it is hard to fake.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Group them by the job they do:</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Keeping the connection when you take state apart</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>toRef(obj, 'key')</code> — a ref bound to one property of a reactive object; writes flow both ways.</li>`,
        `<li><code>toRefs(obj)</code> — the same for every property, so the object can be destructured safely.</li>`,
        `<li><code>toValue(x)</code> (3.3+) — normalises a ref, a getter or a plain value to a value. The standard way for a composable to accept all three.</li>`,
        `<li><code>unref(x)</code> — <code>isRef(x) ? x.value : x</code>. The older, narrower version of <code>toValue</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Opting out of depth (for performance)</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>shallowRef(v)</code> — only <code>.value</code> reassignment is tracked; the contents are not converted.</li>`,
        `<li><code>shallowReactive(o)</code> — only top-level properties are tracked.</li>`,
        `<li><code>triggerRef(r)</code> — manually force a <code>shallowRef</code> to notify after you mutate its contents.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Opting out of reactivity entirely</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>markRaw(o)</code> — permanently exclude an object; <code>reactive()</code> will refuse to wrap it.</li>`,
        `<li><code>toRaw(p)</code> — retrieve the original object behind a proxy.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Restricting writes</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>readonly(o)</code> — a deep read-only proxy; writes warn in development and are ignored.</li>`,
        `<li><code>shallowReadonly(o)</code> — top level only.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Inspection</strong>: <code>isRef</code>, <code>isReactive</code>, <code>isReadonly</code>, <code>isProxy</code>.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch4-reactivity-utilities.svg|Grouped map of Vue reactivity utilities by purpose: connection-preserving toRef and toRefs, depth control with shallowRef and shallowReactive, escape hatches markRaw and toRaw, and write control with readonly|The utilities sorted by the problem they solve: keep the connection, control the depth, escape entirely, or restrict writes.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong><code>toRef</code>/<code>toRefs</code></strong> create refs whose getter and setter proxy to the source object's property. That is why the connection survives: <code>state.count</code> and <code>toRef(state, 'count').value</code> read and write the same slot. <code>toRefs</code> is why the convention "composables return refs" works even when the composable holds a <code>reactive</code> object internally — return <code>toRefs(state)</code> and consumers can destructure.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>shallowRef</code></strong> changes the cost model. A normal <code>ref</code> holding a 10,000-item array converts every item into a reactive proxy the first time it is read. If you only ever <em>replace</em> the whole array, that work buys nothing. <code>shallowRef</code> skips it: only <code>.value = newArray</code> triggers. If you must mutate the contents in place, <code>triggerRef()</code> tells Vue to notify anyway.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>markRaw</code></strong> sets a hidden flag that makes <code>reactive()</code> return the object untouched. This matters more than it sounds. Proxying a class instance from a third-party library — a Chart.js chart, a Leaflet map, a Three.js scene, a WebSocket — can break it, because such objects often rely on internal identity checks, private fields or <code>this</code>-binding that a Proxy interferes with, and deep conversion of a large object graph is expensive besides. Component definitions are another case: storing one in a plain <code>ref</code> proxies the entire definition for no benefit, which is why <code>shallowRef</code> or <code>markRaw</code> is the right home for <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> targets.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>toRaw</code></strong> returns the underlying object. Use it when passing data to something outside Vue — a structured clone, an IndexedDB write, a library that inspects prototypes — or when you need identity comparison. Note that mutating the raw object does <em>not</em> trigger anything, which makes <code>toRaw</code> a footgun if used casually.</p>`,
        `<p><strong><code>readonly</code></strong> wraps a proxy in another proxy that rejects writes. It is deep and it is <em>live</em> — the readonly view reflects changes made through the original. That is the pattern for provide/inject and for exposing store state: hand out a readonly view plus the functions allowed to mutate it.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { reactive, toRef, toRefs, toValue, isRef } from 'vue'\n\nconst state = reactive({ count: 0, name: 'Ada' })\n\n// ❌ plain destructuring loses the connection\nlet { count } = state\ncount++                          // dead\n\n// ✅ toRefs — every property becomes a connected ref\nconst { count: countRef, name: nameRef } = toRefs(state)\ncountRef.value++                 // state.count is now 1\nstate.count++                    // countRef.value is now 2 — both directions\n\n// ✅ toRef for one property; also works for props\nconst justCount = toRef(state, 'count')\n\n// toValue: accept ref | getter | value in a composable\nfunction useDoubled(input) {\n  return computed(() =&gt; toValue(input) * 2)\n}\nuseDoubled(2)              // plain\nuseDoubled(countRef)       // ref\nuseDoubled(() =&gt; state.count)  // getter</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// A composable holding reactive state internally but returning refs\nexport function usePagination() {\n  const state = reactive({ page: 1, perPage: 20, total: 0 })\n  const pages = computed(() =&gt; Math.ceil(state.total / state.perPage))\n\n  function next() { if (state.page &lt; pages.value) state.page++ }\n\n  // toRefs so consumers can destructure safely\n  return { ...toRefs(state), pages, next }\n}\n\nconst { page, total, pages, next } = usePagination()   // all still reactive</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Shallow, for performance:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { shallowRef, triggerRef, shallowReactive } from 'vue'\n\n// A 50k-row dataset that is always replaced wholesale.\n// A deep ref would proxy every row on first read — pure waste.\nconst rows = shallowRef([])\nrows.value = await fetchRows()      // ✅ triggers\nrows.value[0].name = 'x'            // ❌ does NOT trigger (contents untracked)\ntriggerRef(rows)                    // ✅ force a notification if you must mutate\n\n// Top-level only\nconst config = shallowReactive({ theme: 'dark', nested: { deep: 1 } })\nconfig.theme = 'light'              // ✅ triggers\nconfig.nested.deep = 2              // ❌ does not</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>markRaw</code> — the third-party-library case:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { markRaw, reactive, shallowRef, toRaw } from 'vue'\n\n// ❌ Vue proxies the whole map instance — slow, and can break internal identity checks\nconst state = reactive({ map: new LeafletMap(el) })\n\n// ✅ Keep it out of the reactivity system entirely\nconst state = reactive({ map: markRaw(new LeafletMap(el)) })\n\n// ✅ Same idea for component definitions in &lt;component :is&gt;\nconst current = shallowRef(markRaw(ProfileTab))\n\n// toRaw — hand the plain object to something outside Vue\nconst plain = toRaw(state)\nstructuredClone(plain)              // proxies can't always be cloned\nindexedDbStore.put(toRaw(record))\n\n// ⚠️ Mutating the raw object triggers nothing\ntoRaw(state).count++                // invisible to the UI</code></pre>`,
        `<p><code>readonly</code> — the controlled-mutation pattern:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { reactive, readonly, provide } from 'vue'\n\nconst state = reactive({ user: null, token: null })\n\nfunction login(user, token) { state.user = user; state.token = token }\nfunction logout() { state.user = null; state.token = null }\n\n// Descendants can read everything and mutate nothing\nprovide('auth', { state: readonly(state), login, logout })\n\n// In a consumer:\nconst auth = inject('auth')\nauth.state.user            // ✅ reads, and stays live\nauth.state.user = { … }    // ❌ warns in dev, ignored\nauth.login(u, t)           // ✅ the sanctioned path</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for <code>toRefs</code> to fix a problem <code>ref</code> would not have had.</strong> If you default to <code>ref</code>, most of this never comes up.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating a <code>shallowRef</code>'s contents and expecting an update.</strong> Only <code>.value</code> assignment triggers; call <code>triggerRef()</code> if you must mutate in place.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Mutating through <code>toRaw</code>.</strong> No traps fire, so the UI silently ignores it. Use it to <em>read</em> or to hand data out, not to write.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>markRaw</code> to be reversible.</strong> It is permanent for that object; there is no unmark.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>markRaw</code> on an object nested inside an already-reactive one.</strong> The flag is checked at conversion time; marking after the parent was converted may be too late.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Treating <code>readonly</code> as a security boundary.</strong> It is a development-time guard — the warning is stripped in production, and <code>toRaw</code> bypasses it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting that <code>readonly</code> is a different object.</strong> <code>readonly(state) !== state</code>, so identity checks fail.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not using <code>toValue</code> in composable arguments.</strong> Forces every caller to hand over a plain value, so nothing refetches when the input changes.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Default to <code>ref</code> and you will need <code>toRefs</code> only at the boundary of code you did not write.</li>`,
        `<li>Return <code>toRefs(state)</code> from composables that hold a <code>reactive</code> object internally.</li>`,
        `<li>Accept <code>MaybeRefOrGetter</code> arguments in composables and normalise with <code>toValue()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>shallowRef</code> for large immutable payloads replaced wholesale — measure first, but the win on big datasets is real.</li>`,
        `<li>Always <code>markRaw</code> third-party class instances: maps, charts, editors, sockets, observers.</li>`,
        `<li>Store component definitions in <code>shallowRef</code>/<code>markRaw</code>, never a deep <code>ref</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Expose <code>readonly()</code> state plus explicit mutators from stores and providers.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>toRaw</code> when handing data to non-Vue code — cloning, serialising, IndexedDB, canvas libraries.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does <code>toRefs</code> do and when do you need it?"</strong><br>A: It converts every property of a reactive object into a ref that proxies to that property, so the object can be destructured without losing reactivity. You need it whenever reactive state has to be spread or destructured — most commonly when a composable holds a <code>reactive</code> object internally but must return something consumers can destructure. <code>toRef</code> does the same for a single property.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When would you use <code>shallowRef</code>?"</strong><br>A: When the value is large and you always replace it rather than mutate it — a fetched dataset, a parsed document, a chart configuration. A deep <code>ref</code> converts every nested object into a proxy on first read, which for tens of thousands of rows is real time and memory spent on tracking you will never use. <code>shallowRef</code> tracks only reassignment of <code>.value</code>; if you do need to mutate in place, call <code>triggerRef()</code> to notify manually.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is <code>markRaw</code> for?"</strong><br>A: Permanently excluding an object from the reactivity system. The usual case is a class instance from a third-party library — a Leaflet map, a Chart.js chart, a Three.js scene, a WebSocket. Wrapping those in a Proxy is expensive and can genuinely break them, because they rely on internal identity checks, private fields or <code>this</code> binding. Component definitions stored for <code>&lt;component :is&gt;</code> are the other common case.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "<code>readonly</code> — is that real protection?" (trap)</strong><br>A: No. It is a development-time guard: writes warn and are ignored, but the warning is stripped in production and <code>toRaw()</code> gets you the mutable original. Its value is design communication and catching mistakes early — the pattern is to provide <code>readonly(state)</code> alongside the mutator functions, so every write funnels through one auditable place. It is not a security boundary.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the difference between <code>unref</code> and <code>toValue</code>?"</strong><br>A: <code>unref(x)</code> returns <code>x.value</code> if <code>x</code> is a ref, otherwise <code>x</code>. <code>toValue</code> (3.3+) does that <em>and</em> calls <code>x</code> if it is a function, so it normalises refs, getters and plain values alike. That makes it the right tool for composable arguments — the caller can pass a constant, a ref, or a getter over a prop, and the composable works with all three.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>toRef(obj,'k')</code> / <code>toRefs(obj)</code> — keep the two-way connection through destructuring.</li>`,
        `<li><code>toValue(x)</code> — ref | getter | value → value. <code>unref</code> is the older, getter-less version.</li>`,
        `<li><code>shallowRef</code> — only <code>.value</code> reassignment tracked. <code>triggerRef()</code> to force a notify.</li>`,
        `<li><code>shallowReactive</code> — top-level properties only.</li>`,
        `<li><code>markRaw(o)</code> — permanently non-reactive. Use for library instances and component definitions.</li>`,
        `<li><code>toRaw(p)</code> — the original object. Read/serialise with it; <strong>writes trigger nothing</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>readonly(o)</code> — deep, live, dev-only guard. Not security. <code>readonly(x) !== x</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Checks: <code>isRef</code> · <code>isReactive</code> · <code>isReadonly</code> · <code>isProxy</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Compose: <code>readonly(state)</code> + mutators for stores/providers; <code>toRefs(state)</code> for composable returns.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'nextTick and the Async Update Queue',
      slug: 'nexttick-async-updates',
      order: 4,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>"I changed the data but the DOM still has the old value" is a question every Vue developer asks once and then never forgets. The answer — updates are batched and asynchronous — is a small piece of knowledge with a large blast radius: it explains <code>nextTick</code>, it explains why measuring an element right after a state change fails, and it is the reason Vue can handle a thousand mutations in a loop without a thousand renders.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers like it because the naive mental model (state change → DOM change, immediately) is so intuitive and so wrong, and because the correct answer requires understanding the microtask queue — which doubles as a JavaScript fundamentals check.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>When you change reactive state, Vue does <strong>not</strong> re-render immediately. It puts the affected effects on a queue and flushes that queue on the next <strong>microtask</strong>. Until then, the DOM still shows the previous state.</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const count = ref(0)\n\ncount.value = 1\nconsole.log(el.textContent)   // "0" — the DOM has NOT updated yet\n\nawait nextTick()\nconsole.log(el.textContent)   // "1" — now it has</code></pre>`,
        `<p><strong><code>nextTick()</code></strong> returns a promise that resolves once the queue has flushed and the DOM reflects every pending change. It is not a timer and not a delay — it is "after the update that is already scheduled."</p>`,
        `<p>The reason for the design is efficiency. Consider:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">for (let i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) items.value.push(i)</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Synchronous rendering would produce a thousand renders and a thousand DOM patches. Batching produces one. The queue also de-duplicates, so a component whose state changed five times renders once.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Three queues are involved, flushed in order:</p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li><strong>Pre-flush</strong> callbacks — <code>watch</code> with the default <code>flush: 'pre'</code>. These run before rendering, so a watcher can change more state and still be batched into the same render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>The main job queue</strong> — component render effects, <em>sorted by component id</em> so parents render before children. That ordering matters: if a parent's update removes a child, the child should never render at all.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Post-flush</strong> callbacks — <code>watch</code> with <code>flush: 'post'</code>, <code>onUpdated</code> hooks, and the callbacks queued by <code>nextTick()</code>. By now the DOM is patched.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `{{fig:ch4-update-queue.svg|Diagram of the Vue update queue: synchronous state mutations queue effects, then on the next microtask pre-flush watchers run, then component renders sorted parent-first, then post-flush watchers, onUpdated and nextTick callbacks|One microtask, three phases: pre-flush watchers, parent-first renders, then post-flush callbacks and nextTick.}}`,
        `<p>The flush is scheduled with <code>Promise.then()</code> — a <strong>microtask</strong>, not <code>setTimeout</code> (a macrotask). Microtasks drain before the browser paints, so Vue's update lands in the same frame as the change that caused it. There is no visible delay; "asynchronous" here means "after the current synchronous block," not "later in time."</p>`,
        `<p>Two consequences worth knowing. First, an <code>await nextTick()</code> is enough to see the DOM, but if you need a value after <em>layout</em> — a measured height after fonts and images settle — you may still need <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>. Second, because <code>nextTick</code> callbacks run in the post-flush phase, they see the same DOM state that <code>flush: 'post'</code> watchers and <code>onUpdated</code> see.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>When you actually need it</strong> is narrower than people assume: measuring or scrolling a newly rendered element, focusing an input that a <code>v-if</code> just created, calling a third-party library that must see the final DOM, and in tests, where you assert on rendered output. Everything else is usually solved better by a <code>flush: 'post'</code> watcher.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, nextTick } from 'vue'\n\nconst messages = ref([])\nconst listEl = ref(null)\nconst showInput = ref(false)\nconst inputEl = ref(null)\n\n// ① Scroll to the bottom AFTER the new message is rendered\nasync function addMessage(text) {\n  messages.value.push({ id: crypto.randomUUID(), text })\n\n  // ❌ Without this the height is measured before the new row exists,\n  //    so the list scrolls one message short — the classic chat bug.\n  await nextTick()\n\n  listEl.value.scrollTop = listEl.value.scrollHeight\n}\n\n// ② Focus an element that a v-if just created\nasync function toggleInput() {\n  showInput.value = true\n  await nextTick()            // the &lt;input&gt; does not exist until now\n  inputEl.value.focus()\n}\n\n// ③ Callback form (older style, still supported)\nfunction addAndMeasure() {\n  items.value.push(newItem)\n  nextTick(() =&gt; console.log(listEl.value.offsetHeight))\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div ref="listEl" class="messages"&gt;\n    &lt;p v-for="m in messages" :key="m.id"&gt;{{ m.text }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;input v-if="showInput" ref="inputEl"&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Batching, demonstrated:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const count = ref(0)\n\n// All three writes collapse into ONE re-render.\nfunction bump() {\n  count.value++\n  count.value++\n  count.value++\n  console.log(count.value)      // 3 — state is synchronous\n  console.log(el.textContent)   // \"0\" — the DOM is not\n}\n\n// A thousand pushes → one render, one patch\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++) items.value.push(i)</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Where a watcher beats <code>nextTick</code>:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ⚠️ Works, but every caller must remember the await\nasync function select(id) {\n  selectedId.value = id\n  await nextTick()\n  highlightEl.value.scrollIntoView()\n}\n\n// ✅ Declare it once — fires after every DOM update caused by selectedId\nwatch(selectedId, () =&gt; {\n  highlightEl.value?.scrollIntoView()\n}, { flush: 'post' })</code></pre>`,
        `<p>In tests — the most common place you will type it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { mount } from '@vue/test-utils'\nimport { nextTick } from 'vue'\n\nit('renders the new item', async () =&gt; {\n  const wrapper = mount(TodoList)\n\n  await wrapper.find('input').setValue('Buy milk')\n  await wrapper.find('form').trigger('submit')\n\n  // Vue Test Utils' trigger/setValue return nextTick, so \`await\` is enough.\n  // Without it, the assertion runs against the pre-update DOM.\n  expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('Buy milk')\n\n  store.count++\n  await nextTick()               // explicit, for changes made outside the wrapper\n  expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('1')\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Reading the DOM immediately after a state change.</strong> You get the previous frame. This is the bug <code>nextTick</code> exists for.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Confusing state timing with DOM timing.</strong> <code>count.value</code> is updated synchronously; only the <em>DOM</em> is deferred.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>setTimeout(…, 0)</code> instead.</strong> It is a macrotask, so it runs after the microtask queue — it usually works by accident and is not guaranteed to be the right ordering.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>await</code>.</strong> <code>nextTick()</code> without awaiting it does nothing useful.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Sprinkling <code>nextTick</code> everywhere.</strong> If you need it in five places for the same state, a <code>flush: 'post'</code> watcher expresses it once.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting it to wait for layout, images or fonts.</strong> It waits for Vue's patch, not for the browser to finish laying out. Use <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> or <code>ResizeObserver</code> for that.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting it to wait for async children.</strong> A <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> boundary or async component may still be resolving.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Missing it in tests.</strong> Assertions against the pre-update DOM fail confusingly; Vue Test Utils returns <code>nextTick</code> from its interaction helpers, so <code>await</code> them.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use the awaited form — <code>await nextTick()</code> — over the callback form; it reads linearly.</li>`,
        `<li>Prefer a <code>flush: 'post'</code> watcher when the same DOM-dependent effect should follow every change to a value.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep <code>nextTick</code> for genuinely one-off, imperative moments: focus after reveal, scroll after append, measure after render.</li>`,
        `<li>Batch related mutations in a single function and let the queue collapse them — do not try to optimise by hand.</li>`,
        `<li>In tests, await every interaction helper and add an explicit <code>await nextTick()</code> after changes made outside the component.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> when you need post-layout measurements, and <code>ResizeObserver</code> when you need them repeatedly.</li>`,
        `<li>Remember <code>onUpdated</code>, post-flush watchers and <code>nextTick</code> all observe the same phase — pick the one that expresses intent most clearly.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why doesn't the DOM update immediately after I change state?"</strong><br>A: Vue batches updates. A write triggers the dependent effects onto a queue rather than running them, and the queue is de-duplicated and flushed on the next microtask. So the state itself is updated synchronously — reading the ref gives the new value — but the DOM still shows the previous render until the flush. Without batching, a loop with a thousand mutations would cause a thousand renders.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What exactly is <code>nextTick</code>?"</strong><br>A: A promise that resolves after the pending update queue has flushed and the DOM reflects all current state. It is scheduled as a microtask via <code>Promise.then</code>, so it resolves before the browser paints — "asynchronous" means after the current synchronous block, not later in wall-clock time. Use it when you need to read or manipulate DOM that a state change just produced.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Walk through the flush order."</strong><br>A: Three phases in one microtask. First pre-flush callbacks — default <code>watch</code> — which run before rendering, so a watcher that changes more state still lands in the same render. Then the main job queue: component render effects sorted by component id, so parents render before children, which prevents a child from rendering when its parent's update was about to remove it. Then post-flush: <code>flush: 'post'</code> watchers, <code>onUpdated</code> hooks, and <code>nextTick</code> callbacks — all of which see the patched DOM.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Microtask or macrotask, and why does it matter?"</strong><br>A: Microtask, via <code>Promise.then</code>. Microtasks drain before the browser paints, so the update lands in the same frame and there is no visible flicker. Using <code>setTimeout(…, 0)</code> instead schedules a macrotask, which runs after the microtask queue and after a potential paint — it appears to work but gives you the wrong ordering guarantees.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "You push to a chat list and scroll to the bottom, but it stops one message short. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: <code>scrollHeight</code> is read before the new message is rendered, so it is the height of the previous list. Awaiting <code>nextTick()</code> after the push — or moving the scroll into a <code>flush: 'post'</code> watcher on the messages array — makes the measurement happen after the DOM patch.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "<code>nextTick</code> or a post-flush watcher?"</strong><br>A: Same phase, different ergonomics. <code>nextTick</code> is imperative and one-off — good inside a specific handler. A <code>flush: 'post'</code> watcher is declarative and fires after <em>every</em> DOM update caused by the watched value, so no caller has to remember the await. If the same effect follows a value everywhere, the watcher is the better expression.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>State updates are <strong>synchronous</strong>; DOM updates are <strong>batched and async</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Flush is scheduled as a <strong>microtask</strong> (<code>Promise.then</code>) — same frame, before paint.</li>`,
        `<li>Order: pre-flush watchers → render effects (<strong>parent before child</strong>) → post-flush watchers / <code>onUpdated</code> / <code>nextTick</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Queue de-duplicates ⇒ N mutations = 1 render.</li>`,
        `<li><code>await nextTick()</code> = "the DOM now reflects my change."</li>`,
        `<li>Need it for: focus after <code>v-if</code>, scroll after append, measure after render, tests.</li>`,
        `<li>Repeated need for the same value ⇒ use <code>watch(..., { flush: 'post' })</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Doesn't wait for layout/fonts/images (use <code>rAF</code>) or async children (<code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>).</li>`,
        `<li>Never substitute <code>setTimeout(…, 0)</code> — macrotask, wrong ordering.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
